
-- fsck_hfs (Cache Bug Fix Fork) --

This fork patches a cache-corruption bug in BTCheckUnusedNodes that caused
fsck_hfs to hang or produce incorrect results on certain HFS+ volumes.  The
function has been rewritten to bypass the block cache entirely and read unused
B-tree nodes directly from disk.  See CHANGES.md for a full change log.


-- WARNING: Read-Only Mode Required --

ALWAYS run the patched fsck_hfs with the -n flag (no-write / dry-run mode):

    sudo fsck_hfs -n /dev/diskXsY

The fix does NOT handle non-contiguous (fragmented) file extents correctly.
When MapFileBlockC reports contiguousBytes < nodeSize it means the target node
spans two or more extents that are not laid out consecutively on disk.  In that
case the raw read will cover only the first fragment and the remaining bytes
will be garbage, potentially misidentifying a valid node as corrupt or writing
incorrect data back to disk.

For the most common failure case (the Attributes B-tree on a single-extent
volume) this is not a problem.  But on fragmented volumes the -n flag is the
only safe option until full fragmented-extent support is implemented.

Using fsck_hfs without -n on a fragmented volume could cause data corruption
or loss.  You have been warned.


-- How to Build fsck_hfs --

Prerequisites:
  * macOS with Xcode command-line tools installed
  * A public Xcode SDK (macosx)

Option 1 — Make (recommended for standalone builds):

    cd hfs-src-main/
    make            # produces fsck_hfs/fsck_hfs
    make clean      # removes objects and the binary

Option 2 — Xcode:

    xcodebuild -target fsck_hfs -configuration Debug

    If the binary is killed immediately on launch (zsh: killed) open the
    project settings for the fsck_hfs target, go to Build Settings →
    Code Signing Entitlements and clear the value for the macOS SDK.
    See CHANGES.md § "Empty macOS entitlements" for details.


-- How to Use fsck_hfs --

Identify the HFS+ partition device node:

    diskutil list

Unmount the volume first (do not eject — the disk must remain accessible):

    diskutil unmount /dev/diskXsY

Run in read-only mode (safe, recommended):

    sudo fsck_hfs -n /dev/diskXsY

Run with verbose output:

    sudo fsck_hfs -n -f -d /dev/diskXsY

Repair mode (only after understanding the fragmented-extent caveat above and
confirming the volume is not fragmented):

    sudo fsck_hfs -fy /dev/diskXsY

Remount when done:

    diskutil mount /dev/diskXsY

